WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.650
Gate 5 also sets the conventions the
device and server tracks build on.

2
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.845
Device attributes come from device
provisioning, never from the user,

3
00:00:08.845 --> 00:00:12.100
and a device rule can never
read its owner's attributes.

4
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.671
So the provisioning service writes tier,

5
00:00:14.671 --> 00:00:19.662
role and environment to the device's own
slots at registration, running app-only.

6
00:00:19.662 --> 00:00:23.988
Use a dynamic device group only where
the rule has to read those slots.

7
00:00:23.988 --> 00:00:28.247
Everywhere else, use an assignment
filter on Intune's own properties,

8
00:00:28.247 --> 00:00:30.638
like ownership or enrollment profile.

9
00:00:30.638 --> 00:00:33.487
A filter on a slot
simply doesn't evaluate.

10
00:00:33.487 --> 00:00:38.610
For anything time-sensitive, assign to a
user group or All devices with a filter,

11
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.998
and never use a dynamic
device group as an exclusion,

12
00:00:41.998 --> 00:00:45.387
because late membership
lets the policy land first.

13
00:00:45.387 --> 00:00:50.623
VK-INT-01 flags managed
devices missing their slots,

14
00:00:50.623 --> 00:00:57.076
and VK-INT-04 flags any exclusion
built on a dynamic device group.

15
00:00:57.076 --> 00:01:00.038
For servers, Arc tags
reuse the same codes,

16
00:01:00.038 --> 00:01:03.410
and resource groups are
the delegation scope.
